---
title: "A draggable point"
---

`{{< input type="point" >}}` is a two-dimensional coordinate the reader drags. It publishes
`{x, y}` — the one structured value in the input vocabulary — with **y increasing upward**,
because these pads place data points on plots.

{{< input name="p" type="point" min="-3" max="3" step="0.2" value="0.8,1.2" label="new observation" >}}

The pad is focusable and the arrow keys move the point by `step`, so it is operable without
a pointer; the readout beside it is a live region, so the new coordinates are announced.

```{js}
//| name: fitted
//| input: p
const rng = num.random(7);
const base = rng.sample(40, () => [rng.normal(0, 1), rng.normal(0, 1)]);
const p = tali.value("p");
const pts = base.concat([[p.x, p.y]]);
const mx = num.mean(pts.map((d) => d[0]));
const my = num.mean(pts.map((d) => d[1]));
const cov = [
  [num.variance(pts.map((d) => d[0])), num.mean(pts.map((d) => (d[0] - mx) * (d[1] - my)))],
  [num.mean(pts.map((d) => (d[0] - mx) * (d[1] - my))), num.variance(pts.map((d) => d[1]))],
];
return { pts, mean: [mx, my], cov };
```

```{js}
//| label: fig-cloud
//| fig-cap: The cloud refits as the point moves; the dragged observation is the filled mark.
//| input: fitted
const f = tali.get("fitted");
return Plot.plot({
  height: 260,
  x: { domain: [-3.2, 3.2] },
  y: { domain: [-3.2, 3.2] },
  marks: [
    Plot.dot(f.pts.slice(0, -1), { x: (d) => d[0], y: (d) => d[1], stroke: "currentColor" }),
    Plot.dot(f.pts.slice(-1), { x: (d) => d[0], y: (d) => d[1], fill: "currentColor", r: 6 }),
    Plot.dot([f.mean], { x: (d) => d[0], y: (d) => d[1], symbol: "plus", r: 8, stroke: "currentColor" }),
  ],
});
```

```{js}
//| input: fitted
return tali.tex(tali.get("fitted").cov, { label: "Σ̂" });
```

Because the value is an ordinary reactive node, the URL fragment captures it like any
other control: drag the point, copy the address, and the link reopens on @fig-cloud exactly
as it stands.
